  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1019 PM MDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nHAZARDOUS WEATHER POSSIBLE THROUGH FRIDAY: \n\nRemnant tropical moisture will once again take aim at the\nIntermountain West and Rockies. The first wave of this deeper \nmoisture arrives to the Four Corners region by sunrise and \ntrack northeastward across the remainder of eastern Utah and \nwestern Colorado through the day before being swept away on \nFriday. A full array of hazards are possible with this \nprolonged event including river flooding, flash flooding, strong\nto severe thunderstorms, and snow above 12,000-13,000 feet. A \nFlood watch has been issued for parts of southeast Utah and \nsouthwest Colorado beginning 12 PM tomorrow and persisting \nthrough 12 PM Friday.\n\nTomorrow through Thursday:\n\nThe 21/00Z KGJT RAOB is sitting at 2/3 of an inch of PWAT which\nis already anomalous and in the 90th percentile. Impressive for\nmid October but just the tip of the iceberg. GOES All Layer \nPWAT imagery is showing the nose of true tropical mositure \nmoving into south-central AZ this evening and should arrive to \nthe 4 Corners in the pre-sunrise hours. PWAT is projected to \npush above an inch by sunset at GJT which would be a new daily \nrecord and some 300+ percent of normal. So the stage will once \nagain be set for excessive rainfall rates and impressive total \nprecipitation amounts through the event. This moisture will also\neffectively lighten and destabilize the atmosphere and allow \neven minimal lift to produce ascent and efficiently squeeze out \nthis anomalous PWAT. Ascent over the next few days will aided \nby a strong SubTrop jet aloft tomorrow followed by the Polar \njet on Thursday which is associated with the main upstream \ntrough over the West. CAPE will be modest at best over the 4 \nCorners region the next few afternoons. The wind profile will \nhowever be impressive...with low level east/southeast flow \nover-topped by the strong southwest flow aloft. This shear will\nbe favorable for producing periods of organized convection even\nthough plenty of cloud cover will inhibit full surface heating.\nStrong outflow winds will remain the main threat but some \nstorms will also be capable of producing larger hail. The \nveering profile and local terrain effects could also lead to \nsome brief spin ups...not probable but not zero either. Lastly \nthe CAPE profile will be long and skinny...an indication of deep\nwarm cloud processes which favor high precipitation rates as \nwell as high snow levels. It should be no surprise that the \nfertile crescent of the San Juans will also be effective at \nproducing the ascent in this southerly flow pattern. With the \nfire hose pointed directly at them precipitation amounts in the \n2-4 inch range are still forecast with higher amounts possible \ndue to convection. Rain or a nighttime mix at pass level could \ntranslate to 6 to 12 inches or more of snow over the highest \npeaks.\n\nFriday: \n\nUnlike the last event the upstream system will be lifting\nnorthward through the Great Basin instead of sending the main\ncirculation overhead. Drier air will be pulled in as this system\nlifts into the northern Rockies through the day on Friday. A\ndecrease in precipitation coverage and intensity is expected\nthrough the day as PWAT is forecast to crash below a half of an\ninch by midday. Even after it stops raining, the risk for \nflooding will remain elevated on Friday as water continues to \nwork through the system. Some main stem rivers are forecast to\ncrest on Friday morning. We'll have a better idea of how long \nflooding may continue to cause issues after we get a better \nsense of how much rainfall is received on Thursday.\n\n\nLOOKING AHEAD:\n\nChances for light precipitation (valley rain, mountain snow \nabove 10,000 feet) continue through the weekend as a low \npressure system, currently spinning off the California coast, \ntraverses the Intermountain West. Plenty of uncertainty remains \nin how this low will evolve as it moves inland, but ensemble \nguidance tends to show a weakening system by the time it reaches\nour forecast area. Regardless, this system will provide enough \nforcing for scattered to widespread precipitation as it moves \nthrough, alongside a brief reduction in temperatures.",
